==English==
{{wikipedia|carbuncle (disambiguation)}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ONF.|en}} {{m|fro|carbuncle}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈkɑːbʌŋkəl/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈkɑːɹbʌŋkəl/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{label|en|archaic}} A deep-red or fiery colored [[garnet]] or other dark red precious stone, especially when cut [[cabochon]].
#* '''1602''', [[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]], ''[[s:Hamlet|Hamlet]]'', Act II, scene 2, line 401:
#*: With eyes like '''carbuncles''', the hellish Pyrrhus {{...}}
#* '''1611''', ''[[Bible]]'' ({{w|King James Version}}), [[Isaiah]] 54:12:
#*: And I will make thy windows of agates, and thy gates of '''carbuncles''', and all thy borders of pleasant stones.
#* '''1634''', [[w:Sir Thomas Herbert, 1st Baronet|Thomas Herbert]], ''A Relation of Some Yeares Trauaile, Begunne Anno 1626. Into Afrique and the Greater Asia, especially the Territories of the Persian Monarchie: And some Parts of the Orientall Indies, and Iles Adiacent. Of their Religion, Language, Habit, Discent, Ceremonies, and other Matters Concerning Them: Together with the Proceedings and Death of the Three Late Ambassadours: Sir D. C[otton] Sir R. S[herley] and the Persian Nogdi-Beg: As also the Two Great Monarchs, the King of Persia, and the Great Mogol'', London: Printed by {{w|William Stansby}}, and Iacob Bloome, <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/644078533 644078533]</small>; republished as [[w:William Foster (historiographer)|William Foster]], editor, ''Travels in Persia 1627–1629. Abridged and Edited by Sir William Foster'' [...] ''with an Introduction and Notes'' (Broadway Travellers), London: [[w:Routledge|G. Routledge & Sons]], 1928, <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/4900176 4900176]</small>, page 79:
#*: His turban, or mandil [''mandīl''], was of finest white silk interwoven with gold, bestudded with pearl[s] and '''carbuncles'''; {{...}}
# {{label|en|heraldry}} A [[charge]] or [[bearing]] supposed to represent the precious stone, with eight [[sceptre]]s or [[stave#Noun|staves]] [[radiate|radiating]] from a common centre; an [[escarbuncle]].
# An [[abscess]] larger than a [[boil]], usually with one or more openings draining [[pus]] onto the skin. It is usually caused by [[bacterial]] [[infection]].
# An unpopular or ugly building; an [[eyesore]].

